# Contributing to this ontology hub

This managed guide describes how changes are branched and reviewed. See `CICD.md` for
how they are validated, released, promoted, rolled back, and hotfixed.

## Branching

`main` is protected and is the only long-lived branch. Never commit to it directly —
always branch and open a pull request.

| Prefix | Use for |
|---|---|
| `model/<domain>-<topic>` | Ontology, shape, binding, or contracted dbt change |
| `import/<source>-<topic>` | New or corrected source evidence |
| `upgrade/toolkit-<version>` | Toolkit or reference-model upgrade |
| `fix/<topic>` | Normal correction |
| `hotfix/<version>-<topic>` | Urgent correction based on a released hub commit |

Branches should live for days, not weeks. Open a draft PR early when changing a shared
domain, and coordinate with the domain owner before making concurrent edits to it.

## Submitting a change

1. Branch from `main` using the prefix table above.
2. Author the change — authored inputs and any release-relevant generated diff belong
   in the same pull request; never hand-edit compiler-owned output under
   `ontology-hub-publish/`.
3. Run the pull-request checks documented in `CICD.md` before opening the PR.
4. Open the PR against `main`. CI must validate the prospective merge result.
5. Toolkit and reference-model upgrades stay isolated from modeling changes, so
   reviewers can distinguish tool-generated consequences from intentional semantic
   changes.

## Updating this guide

This file and `CICD.md` are toolkit-managed. Adopt a newer version with:

```powershell
uv run kairos-ontology update --upgrade
# On Windows, wait for the detached managed-file refresh to finish.
uv run kairos-ontology update --check
```

If the toolkit pin is already current, run `uv run kairos-ontology update` without
`--upgrade`; plain `update` completes synchronously.
